\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s31 \li1209 \fi-1007 \ri1007 \ql Class Description\
\fs14\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 The NSProtocolChecker class defines an object that restricts the messages that can be sent to another object (referred to as the checker\'27s delegate).  This can be particularly useful when an object with many methods, only a few of which ought to be remotely accessible,  is made available using the Distributed Objects system.\
\fs14\
\fs28 A protocol checker acts as a kind of proxy; when it receives a message that is in its designated protocol, it forwards the message to its delegate, and consequently appears to be the delegate itself.  However, when it receives a message not in its protocol, it raises an NSInvalidArgumentException exception to indicate that the message isn\'27t allowed, whether or not the delegate implements the method.\
\fs14\
\fs28 Typically, an object that is to be distributed (yet must restrict messages) creates an NSProtocolChecker for itself and returns the checker rather than returning itself in response to any messages.  The object might also register the checker as the root object of an NSConnection.\
\fs14\
\fs28 The object should be careful about vending references to \b self\b0 ; the protocol checker will convert a return value of \b self\b0  to indicate the checker rather than the object for any messages that were forwarded by the checker, but direct references to the object (bypassing the checker) could be passed around by other objects.\
\fs14\
